==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|sylky}}, equivalent to {{af|en|silk|-y|id2=adjectival}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪlki/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-silky.wav|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪlki|s=2}}
* {{homophones|en|Sylki}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Similar in [[appearance]] or [[texture]] (especially in [[softness]] and [[smoothness]]) to [[silk]].
#: {{co|en|'''silky''' hair}}
#: {{ux|en|cloth with a '''silky''' lustre}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=The Field and the Heath|title=Beauties and Wonders of Vegetable Life;{{nb...|or, Rambles in Parks, Forests, Conservatories, Orchards, Gardens, Fields, and Heaths. With Numerous Illustrations.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Religious Tract Society|The Religious Tract Society]],{{nb...|56, Paternoster Row; 65, St. Paul’s Churchyard, and 164, Piccadilly; and sold by the booksellers.}}|year=[1866]|page=263|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cFYDAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA263|oclc=63069913|passage=A portion of the Baromez does present a rude resemblance in its shape to the figure of an animal, and is covered by a soft downy substance, which may be compared to a '''silky''' fleece, of a reddish-brown colour, and which gives to it more the appearance of a dog than a lamb.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Thomas Berger (novelist)|Thomas Berger}}|chapter=My Indian Wife|title={{w|Little Big Man (novel)|Little Big Man}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Dial Press|year=1964|page=214|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/littlebigman0000thom/page/214/mode/1up|lccn=64-20284|passage=Then I looked close at the scalp he stroked, which was of the '''silkiest''' blonde. For a moment I was sure it come from Olga’s dear head, and reckoned also he had little Gus’s fine skull-cover someplace among his filthy effects, the stinking old savage, living out his life of murder, rapine, and squalor, and I almost knifed him before I collected myself and realized the hair was honeyer than my Swedish wife’s.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=E. N. Welch|chapter=Just an Unofficial Investigation|editor=Cynthia Manson|title=Murder on Main Street: Small-Town Crime from Ellery Queen’s Mystery Magazine & Alfred Hitchcock’s Mystery Magazine|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Barnes & Noble|Barnes & Noble Books}}|year=1986|year_published=1993|page=238|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/muderonmainstree00cynt/page/238/mode/1up|isbn=1-56619-041-X|passage=The door opened, and there stood Miss Ellerby, looking as Miss Ellerby should, unschoolmarmish and casually chic in a '''silky''' shirt, faded jeans, and running shoes.}}
# {{U|smooth}} and [[pleasant]]; [[seductive]].
#: {{co|en|a '''silky''' wine}}
#: {{co|en|a '''silky''' voice}}
#: {{co|en|'''silky''' skills}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=Gary L. Friedman|title=The Friedman Archives Guide to the Sony RX-10 IV|passage=If you have a circular polarizer or a neutral density (grey) filter, put that in front of the lens too. This will slow down the shutter speed even further to get '''silkier''', smoothier (that's a word!) water motion.}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} Covered in long, slender, glistening hairs pressed close to the surface; [[sericeous]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} Having sensibilities of mainstream culture, in contrast to {{m|en|crunchy}}.
#: {{ux|en|Cribs and formula, strollers and baby food epitomize '''silky''' parenting.}}

